# Exercise: Less-or-Equal and Greater-or-Equal (i.<= and i.>=)
#
# The `i.<=` and `i.>=` operators include equality.
# (Word forms `i.lte` and `i.gte` also work.)
# Seq also supports type-inferred `<=` and `>=` which work for both integers and floats.
#
# Stack effects:
#   i.<= ( a b -- bool )   true if a <= b
#   i.>= ( a b -- bool )   true if a >= b
#
# Examples:
#   5 5 i.<=    # true (5 is less than or equal to 5)  (explicit integer)
#   5 5 <=      # true (5 is less than or equal to 5)  (inferred integer)
#   5 5 i.>=    # true (5 is greater than or equal to 5) (explicit integer)
#   5 5 >=      # true (5 is greater than or equal to 5) (inferred integer)
#   3 5 i.<=    # true (3 is less than or equal to 5) (explicit integer)
#   3 5 <=      # true (3 is less than or equal to 5) (inferred integer)
#   1.5 2.5 <=  # true (inferred float)
#
# Your task: Check if 7 is less than or equal to 7.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-less-equal ( -- )
    # Your code here - is 7 i.<= 7?
    false
    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
